today i'm going to answer your questions  on career and consulting topics  on my instagram channel firm learning i  recently asked you  to submit the questions that you want to  have answered by me  already directly answered many of them  on my instagram channel but still there  were some questions that  many people asked and i want to use this  video to answer these questions for this  broader audience because i know and i  trust that many of you guys are asking  yourself the same questions so welcome  to another coffee break here on my  channel firm learning  my name is heinrich and here on my  channel firm learning i want to help you  to become successful in the first years  of your career so i will now always read  out the questions here on my phone  and the first question is can non-native  germans enter into mckinsey and germany  with c1 level german and here questions  like this now always i will answer with  a more general type of approach so not  talking about specific firms but rather  for instance mbb firms in general and  the answer here is that  yes you do not need to be a native  german to work at a german  mbb firm and this is true for most other  european countries as well  indeed the two requirements that will be  required from you  are first that usually you will need to  have a valid work permit a valid work  visa  to actually be able to work in the  country and second you will need to have  local language skills so not necessarily  on a native level but at least on a  business proficiency level  so c1 should usually qualify for that  next question  is it worth doing an mba even though i  have a phd and three years of consulting  experience  and here now i want to generalize this  to the question is it worth to do an mba  right especially if you already have  lots of academic experience  and here i'd say it very much depends on  your goals  so usually just to advance in your  career i do not think that an mba is  necessarily required however when mba  can help you is to make a transition in  your career  so whenever you feel like you are stuck  in a specific area in your career  then the nba is like the joker card that  you can pull out  once in your life and that can really  help you to make that transition  and already once made a video about that  how exactly this works  i will link it somewhere about field so  check it out to learn more about this  is it possible to get into mbb with an  extremely low  gpa and i have three years of experience  so let's just say it will be extremely  difficult especially if your gpa is  really low so i'd say if your gpa maybe  is average but not super stellar then it  already will be tough  but probably you can compensate that if  some other parts of your profile are  really stellar but if your gpa is  really low then again i don't want to  say it's impossible maybe if you're  really a genius a superstar and this  comes true through your application by  other areas of your profile  then maybe you have a shot but for most  people i consider it  really difficult and probably  unfortunately not realistic how would  you go about getting an internship at  one of the big  four consulting firms and here i don't  think there's some kind of magic trick  that you need to have right and what you  need to understand is that these firms  often have highly professional  recruiting processes so while of course  it might help if you already know a  consultant and he can then directly  forward your application to hr  you should be able to assume that with  these firms you can just apply via the  online form and your application will be  treated professionally and if you  fulfill the criteria then you will be  invited and indeed when i applied to nbb  i always just use the regular  application  form i didn't have any insider contacts  in these firms and this worked out  really well for me  and it will work for you as well if you  fulfill of course the criteria  how hard is it to get a mckinsey after  your bachelor and indeed not  generalizing it not for all the mbb  firms so usually you can assume that if  you have a bachelor the hurdles to get  in  are pretty similar than if you have a  pre-experienced master  therefore it can be a bit tougher to get  in with only a bachelor  because the criteria against you are  measured and very similar compared to if  you start with a master but as it will  be one or two years earlier in your  development both from the personal side  but maybe also from the internships that  you have completed you will need to have  achieved  more in the rather short time span of  the bachelor to be invited to the  interview and then to get in therefore  it's not impossible but yes i guess you  can  argue that it's a bit more challenging  our mba is  mandatory to get into mbb no not at all  this is  not at all mandatory and especially in  countries like germany  mbas are still quite uncommon just most  people don't do an mba  so people are regularly hired at mbb  firms without an mba but even in the us  or other countries where mbas are more  common you can absolutely start with a  bachelor with an undergraduate degree in  a business analyst position and you will  get in  in an mba by no means it's a hard  requirement what is the best book that  i've read  recently so here i can recommend you to  check out my book recommendations you  find a link to that below in the video  description they are linked to a page  with all the books that i can really  recommend  and of course some of them i've also  read recently do you think there might  be a valid reason to go for  tier two instead of a tier one so here  of course people might have very  personal reasons to choose certain firms  and of course personal fit might play a  role as well but i believe that  for most candidates there's not really a  reason why you should join in here too  if you have the opportunity to join a  tier one and the reason is that in terms  of work life balance in terms of overall  working experience they are quite  similar but in tier one firms you will  often have the opportunity  to work for slightly more relevant and  important projects also the support that  you get via the whole back office  infrastructure is larger so you can  focus  more as a consultant on the key  consulting topics and lastly the brand  value for your cv but also your exit  opportunities after your work will be  slightly better  in tier one firms compared to two firms  this is at least my opinion what are  advantages or disadvantages of  doing a master and here i think the  answer is pretty similar to advantages  or disadvantages  of doing an mba and here i also did a  video about that i will link it  somewhere here above but in general the  cost of doing a master are twofold first  it's an investment of time and second  it's an investment of  money so you need to ask yourself  whether these two types of investments  are worth it the investment of time will  usually be compensated if you're able to  start one step  higher if you start in the workforce  with the master compared to a bachelor  and at least for all the consulting  firms this is usually the case  usually you start one level higher with  a master  and therefore you can compensate for the  loss of time by already being one step  further ahead in your career journey  especially if you work in a private  university you will need to pay student  fees  but then the second factor of it is also  that in this time  you will not earn money right so you  miss the salary so this of course can  add up to quite a high sum but again if  the master opens up career opportunities  for you which might have not been  available with your bachelor or maybe  also the knowledge that you acquire via  your master will then really help you to  be successful in the long term then this  might easily be  overcompensated throughout the lifespan  of your career so keep these two things  in mind but my personal opinion is that  if you do a master at a target  university with good grades  usually this will be worth the  investment in the long term in most  cases  what do i do for a living did i quit  consulting enough yes  why so yes i quit consulting about a  year ago i worked with mckinsey but now  left the firm  since then and the reasons i detailed in  another video where i talk about what  things  are that i didn't learn in consulting  and i will also link it somewhere above  so overall i'm super grateful and  thankful for my time at mbb it was an  amazing experience and i do believe that  consulting is a great career choice for  many young business graduates out there  but still i've heard that after these  years i wanted to do something slightly  more operational so i joined a german  retail company  in a cto position so i'm working on this  new career since about a year  and of course in parallel i know also  started this youtube channel if you have  more questions that now were not  answered here in this video feel free to  leave me a comment below in the comment  section  and i will do my very best to answer  every single one if you took  any value out of this video at all  please smash the like button for the  youtube algorithm  and also subscribe to this channel to  stay up to date on my content i release  weekly videos  every single saturday if you want to see  even more from me and potentially also  contribute to future q a sessions  you can follow me on my instagram my  handle is firm learning and i also have  a mailing list you can find a sign up  link to that mailing list below in the  video description  i want to take a moment also thank all  the sponsors of my channel  without all of you guys it wouldn't be  possible what i do so  thank you so much and if you are  thinking about becoming a sponsor as  well  and get access to some great member only  sponsor only perks  you can sign up via the join button that  is next to the subscribe button so have  a look at that i'd love you to become a  member and i would be super thankful for  your support so big thanks to all of you  for watching i very much appreciate it  and i see you again  next saturday bye

